A||||11||fiero56||Engine Dilema||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-23-2000||10:15 PM||||I have had my mind set on installing a 3.4 rebuild in my Fiero for much of this year. The plan was to buy the engine this month, and begin the rebuild. Here is the situation. My Formula has 165K miles, and an engine with a rod knocking. I have since purchased an '86 2.8 with 140K miles, with plans to replace the bottom end of the engine, and stick it in my Formula until the 3.4 project is completed. This will leave me with my stock 2.8 sitting in the garage, with no plans for it, and less money since I bought the other 2.8. <BR>I got to thinking today. Rather than have 3 engines, and only one Fiero, what if I just do a 3.2 rebuild on my '88 2.8 while it is out of the car. I could do a few mods, and put in a few goodies. I don't care much about HP, but absolutely LOVE an engine that puts out lots of TORQUE! How much of a drop in torque should I expect if I put in a 3.2 rebuild, rather than a 3.5? I don't have Desktop Dyno(yet), but am curious if I could put in a 3.2 and not have a noticeable drop in performance from the 3.5. This would also save me a good $1000, maybe more. Just speculation.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||09-24-2000||01:32 PM||||Here is what I got on dyno2000. I used the GM performance cam with .427/.454 lift and 204/216 duration at .050" lift. The 3.2 has 8.8 compression and the 3.4 has 9.0 compression with the stock fiero heads.<P>RPM 3.2 torque        3.4 torque<BR>2000--182         199<BR>2500--189         205<BR>3000--205         215 <BR>3500--201         213<BR>4000--197         207<BR>4500--186         194<BR>5000--173         178<BR>5500--155         156<P>The difference in the acceleration would be <BR>14.6 Vs 14.5 in the quarter mile and 6.4 Vs 6.2 to 60 MPH with a 2925 lb. fiero including the driver.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-24-2000||01:33 PM||||I'm with you on the torque thing. 'Tweach his own, but there's just no substitute for plenty torque, in my book.<BR> <BR>To complete the cost-benefit analysis, you'd have to estimate how much $ you could sell the "extra" 2.8 motor for.  For example, if you know someone in your area that wants to replace their dead Duck with a V6, you might have a ready-made customer.<P>On the other hand, if you believe that $1000 cost savings figure, it's pretty hard to ignore that. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||09-24-2000||05:08 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>Those figures are sweet! I wasn't even thinking about torque readings until now. I wasn't expecting a very large increase, but I think adding at least 30 ft lbs of torque sounds awsome. Plus, 0-60 in 6.4 sounds pretty tempting, even though 6.2 seconds to 60 sounds even better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I will think about it for about a week, and hear what others have to say, but I think a 3.2 is the way to go for me.<P><BR>I thought about selling the old 2.8 from my '88, but figured I wouldn't mind keeping it, rebuilding it to 3.2 liters and storing it until I came upon another Fiero. Then realized, I could rebuild it, put it back in, and save at least $1000 from a 3.4 right now.<P>The temporary engine I have bought, will most likely be for sale when I compete the 3.2 liter build.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||09-24-2000||06:29 PM||||fiero56,  I am in a similar situation.  Just yesterday I was starting to tear down the top end of my kitcar project 2.8 to just seal it up and use it until it dies and can be replaced with a 3.8SC or V8.  Well, I got the first valve cover off and it looks like mud was used for oil.  So, now I have to drop the cradle and either rebuild the 2.8, throw in a 3.4/3.5 and put a supercharger on it later, or bit the bullet and get a V8.  In any case it is going to be some bucks to complete.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||09-24-2000||10:51 PM||||For some reason the few people here who have the 3.2-liter engine don't get those performance numbers. I some cases there cars are no faster than a slightly modified 2.8 is. I have yet to here from someone on this forum say that there 3.2-liter fiero can go the quarter mile in 14.6 and 0-60 in 6.4 seconds. If someone’s fiero does do this please speak up. On the other hand the one's with the 3.4-liter engines usually get performance numbers pretty close to what the Dragstrip2000 program has come up with. For example I remember a post by Cliff Pennock himself who test drove a 3.4-liter fiero that had a G-tech in it and he got 0-60 in 6.2 seconds.<P>No one really knows which pistons give you the correct compression ratio for the 3.2 buildup. Some have told me that the Sil-O-lite 1486 pistons will give you an 8.8 compression ratio but can't prove it. <P>The good thing about the 3.4-liter engine is that it is an updated design with a better oiling system and stronger block than your 2.8 has. This is why I went with the 3.4-liter engine. Plus every little bit of displacement counts in my book. I would recommend that you give the 3.4-liter engine more thought. You also get the 3.4 injectors when you get a junkyard engine.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||09-25-2000||07:59 AM||||Drew - have you figured out how you're going to get that other engine?  Is John going to deliver it?<P>Also, talk to Mike (BOILERMAKER) about his 3.2 motor.  I got to drive it at the Nationals back in July and it felt REALLY strong.  He could probably give some good suggestions.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||09-25-2000||08:00 AM||||Now you really have me thinkin'. I was all set for the 3.2 build, but I was also thinking the same thing, about other 3.2 builds not living up to their name. My only guess(and I'm no expert by any means)is the larger displacement is only due to the longer stroke, rather than a larger diameter. Affects real world HP/TQ numbers? I don't know, that's all I can think of. The 3.4 uses the same stroke as the 3.1(3.4 uses a 3.1 crank, its even stamped "3.1"), but the diameter of the cylinder is larger. Maybe diameter is a much bigger influence for performance than stroke? I have no idea, this is just speculation unless someone can back it up with proof.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||09-25-2000||08:03 AM||||Hey Scott. Look at our post times. Right now I am planning on using moms minivan, and a VERY clean wooden crate, with plastic over EVERYTHING. I havent proposed my idea yet though <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||09-25-2000||08:38 AM||||Please allow me to further confuse the issue. (I'm good at that, you know.) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I believe that Ed parks said that some (all?) of the reman 3.4's that he is getting have actually been over-bored to 3.5-3.6. (This due to reboring during the rebuilding process.) <BR>Wondering how much could be gotten from one of those with some porting. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||09-25-2000||10:33 AM||||I was planning on having it bored to 3.5. I have heard of Ed being able to get a 3.4 bored out to 3.6, but with no possibility of reboring again for second rebuild. I do wonder what torque numbers a 3.5 would put out.<BR>I'll wait and see what 3.1/3.2 Fiero owners have to say.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BOILERMAKER||09-25-2000||06:46 PM||||OK, here's my take on the subject.  I love my 3.2, but there is always something stronger out there.  What I feel in mine, and I am sure others have different opinions, is that I have a VERY strong 2.8.  Now, keep in mind I never felt what a brand new 2.8 Fiero engine felt like, so I can't really say for a fact that I feel a HUGE increase over the stock engine.<P>If you need verification of the engine, you are welcome to drive it (please overlook the mess of the paint if you don't mind).  You remember the gold car, don't you?  The 3.2 is better than that one, but it isn't a 3800 (drool).<P>If I were to make it as simple as possible, I would have to say that I have a remarkable 2.8 (which IS what it started as, wasn't it?).||||reg||1
